{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15568 - PROTEIN:5826",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate may interact with SMMC-7721, through a series of molecular targets. Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate is directly linked to the CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which is a key enzyme in the sialylation pathway. This enzyme is targeted by multiple compounds, including (1S,3E,4S,5S,6S)-4,5-dihydroxy-3-(3-methylbut-2-enylidene)-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptan-2-one and 1-Hydroxycyclocolorenone, both of which are implicated in targeting SMMC-7721. The shared target of these compounds suggests a possible pathway where Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate may influence the activity of the sialyltransferase, potentially modulating the expression or function of SMMC-7721. The evidence is supported by a consistent link between Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate and SMMC-7721 through a central enzyme, indicating a plausible mechanistic connection. The integration of multiple targets and their shared effect on SMMC-7721 supports the hypothesis that Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate may exert a biological influence on this cell line through the sialyltransferase pathway.",
